<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p><monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> is a Python package for
  cosmological forecasts with Fisher-matrix and Monte Carlo methods.
  Given a fiducial cosmology and a survey specification, it computes the
  observables of galaxy clustering, weak lensing, intensity mapping and
  the cosmic microwave background (CMB), and it returns the expected
  constraints on cosmological parameters, either as a Fisher matrix or
  as a sampled posterior. Previously known as
  <monospace>cosmicfishpie</monospace>, it has been used in about ten
  papers since the Euclid IST:F forecasts
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration et al., 2020" rid="ref-Blanchard2020" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration et al., 2020</xref>).</p>
  <p>Three things are new in this release:</p>
  <list list-type="bullet">
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Speed.</bold> The symbolic emulator
      (<xref alt="Bartlett, Kammerer, et al., 2024" rid="ref-Bartlett2024a" ref-type="bibr">Bartlett,
      Kammerer, et al., 2024</xref>;
      <xref alt="Bartlett, Wandelt, et al., 2024" rid="ref-Bartlett2024b" ref-type="bibr">Bartlett,
      Wandelt, et al., 2024</xref>;
      <xref alt="Sui et al., 2025" rid="ref-Sui2025" ref-type="bibr">Sui
      et al., 2025</xref>) gives Fisher matrices in about 5 seconds.</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Differentiability.</bold> The integration of JAX
      (<xref alt="Bradbury et al., 2018" rid="ref-Bradbury2018" ref-type="bibr">Bradbury
      et al., 2018</xref>) and of the <monospace>jax-cosmo</monospace>
      backend
      (<xref alt="Campagne et al., 2023" rid="ref-Campagne2023" ref-type="bibr">Campagne
      et al., 2023</xref>) allows for autodiff Fisher matrices.</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Fisher and MCMC from the same settings.</bold> This is
      version 2.0 also because MCMC forecasts can now be run with
      exactly the same settings as the Fisher forecast, which allows us
      to test contours that were not obvious before.</p>
    </list-item>
  </list>
  <p>This is also, to our knowledge, the only project that provides a
  CAMB–CLASS validation dashboard for many cosmological models in a FAIR
  way (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable)
  (<xref alt="Wilkinson et al., 2016" rid="ref-Wilkinson2016" ref-type="bibr">Wilkinson
  et al., 2016</xref>), with all the ini files, YAML files and a full
  reproducibility pipeline. A companion DagsHub repository contains the
  validation data for both Fisher matrices and MCMC chains.</p>
  <p>The tentacles of the jellyfish in the logo stand for the chains,
  and the J in the name stands for the new JAX compatibility.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p><monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> is needed to forecast
  constraints for current and future probes, and especially for their
  combinations, in an efficient, validated and reproducible way.</p>
  <p>The code ships survey specifications for Euclid
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration, Mellier, et al., 2025" rid="ref-Mellier2025" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration, Mellier, et al., 2025</xref>), SKAO
  (<xref alt="Square Kilometre Array Cosmology Science Working Group et al., 2020" rid="ref-Bacon2020" ref-type="bibr">Square
  Kilometre Array Cosmology Science Working Group et al., 2020</xref>)
  and Planck-like CMB experiments
  (<xref alt="Planck Collaboration et al., 2020" rid="ref-Planck2018" ref-type="bibr">Planck
  Collaboration et al., 2020</xref>), and it can load user
  specifications for DESI
  (<xref alt="DESI Collaboration et al., 2016" rid="ref-DESI2016" ref-type="bibr">DESI
  Collaboration et al., 2016</xref>) and LSST
  (<xref alt="Ivezić &amp; others, 2019" rid="ref-Ivezic2019" ref-type="bibr">Ivezić
  &amp; others, 2019</xref>). These surveys are far from over: Euclid
  still has about six years of observations ahead and LSST ten, and
  their cross-correlations have barely been studied so far. A
  forecasting code of this kind will therefore remain useful for a long
  time. Moreover, the step from here into real-data analysis is no
  longer a large one.</p>
  <p>The plan for the future is twofold:</p>
  <list list-type="bullet">
    <list-item>
      <p>implement new probes, such as SPHEREx
      (<xref alt="Doré &amp; others, 2014" rid="ref-Dore2014" ref-type="bibr">Doré
      &amp; others, 2014</xref>), WST
      (<xref alt="Mainieri &amp; others, 2024" rid="ref-Mainieri2024" ref-type="bibr">Mainieri
      &amp; others, 2024</xref>) and other futuristic probes, or
      extensions such as SPT-10k <bold>[TODO: cite SPT-10k]</bold>;</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p>implement cross-correlations that have been studied in previous
      papers, such as dark sirens <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\times]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>×</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      Euclid GCph <bold>[TODO: cite Giona’s paper]</bold>.</p>
    </list-item>
  </list>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p><bold>Unvalidated forecasts.</bold> Many forecasts in the
  literature, especially those based on Fisher matrices, are not
  validated. We have seen in the Euclid IST:F paper
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration et al., 2020" rid="ref-Blanchard2020" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration et al., 2020</xref>) and in other works that independent
  pipelines differed by 10% or more, and that it was hard to make them
  agree. The same lesson appears elsewhere: the Fisher approach for
  spectroscopic surveys had to be validated against MCMC
  (<xref alt="Yahia-Cherif et al., 2021" rid="ref-YahiaCherif2021" ref-type="bibr">Yahia-Cherif
  et al., 2021</xref>), numerical derivatives were shown to be a major
  source of instability in LSST-like forecasts
  (<xref alt="Bhandari et al., 2021" rid="ref-Bhandari2021" ref-type="bibr">Bhandari
  et al., 2021</xref>), and the Euclid <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\times]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>×</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  CMB forecasts needed their own cross-validation
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration et al., 2022" rid="ref-Ilic2022" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration et al., 2022</xref>). Recent tools address parts of the
  problem: <monospace>Augur</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Rogozenski et al., 2026" rid="ref-Rogozenski2026" ref-type="bibr">Rogozenski
  et al., 2026</xref>) is the Fisher tool of LSST DESC, built on CCL
  (<xref alt="Chisari et al., 2019" rid="ref-Chisari2019" ref-type="bibr">Chisari
  et al., 2019</xref>) and validated against CosmoSIS
  (<xref alt="Zuntz et al., 2015" rid="ref-Zuntz2015" ref-type="bibr">Zuntz
  et al., 2015</xref>) and nested sampling, and
  <monospace>DerivKit</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Šarčević et al., 2026" rid="ref-Sarcevic2026" ref-type="bibr">Šarčević
  et al., 2026</xref>) provides stable numerical derivatives
  independently of any cosmology code. General samplers such as
  MontePython
  (<xref alt="Audren et al., 2013" rid="ref-Audren2013" ref-type="bibr">Audren
  et al., 2013</xref>;
  <xref alt="Brinckmann &amp; Lesgourgues, 2019" rid="ref-Brinckmann2019" ref-type="bibr">Brinckmann
  &amp; Lesgourgues, 2019</xref>) and Cobaya
  (<xref alt="Torrado &amp; Lewis, 2021" rid="ref-Torrado2021" ref-type="bibr">Torrado
  &amp; Lewis, 2021</xref>) can produce forecasts, but they are not
  Fisher codes.</p>
  <p><bold>CAMB versus CLASS.</bold> Beyond the original comparisons of
  the two codes at the level of spectra
  (<xref alt="Lesgourgues, 2011" rid="ref-Lesgourgues2011" ref-type="bibr">Lesgourgues,
  2011</xref>;
  <xref alt="Lesgourgues &amp; Tram, 2011" rid="ref-LesgourguesTram2011" ref-type="bibr">Lesgourgues
  &amp; Tram, 2011</xref>), almost no one has published validated
  comparisons between CAMB
  (<xref alt="Lewis et al., 2000" rid="ref-Lewis2000" ref-type="bibr">Lewis
  et al., 2000</xref>) and CLASS
  (<xref alt="Blas et al., 2011" rid="ref-Blas2011" ref-type="bibr">Blas
  et al., 2011</xref>) at the level of forecasts, especially in the
  neutrino sector
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025" rid="ref-Archidiacono2025" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025</xref>).
  <monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> therefore also acts as a
  translator between these two codes in a FAIR way, or at least on the
  road to FAIR.</p>
  <p><bold>Autodiff Fisher matrices.</bold>
  <monospace>jax-cosmo</monospace> showcased autodiff Fisher matrices
  and compared them with finite differences from CCL for two parameters
  (<xref alt="Campagne et al., 2023" rid="ref-Campagne2023" ref-type="bibr">Campagne
  et al., 2023</xref>), and differentiable emulators have followed in
  recent years
  (<xref alt="Piras &amp; Spurio Mancini, 2023" rid="ref-Piras2023" ref-type="bibr">Piras
  &amp; Spurio Mancini, 2023</xref>). Here we show, for a full Euclid
  setup with nuisance parameters, that the autodiff Fisher matrix agrees
  well with finite differences of the same observable, and also with a
  non-JAX implementation of the observable.</p>
  <p><bold>An independent testing ground.</bold> Finally, we have
  implemented likelihoods that agree with the direct Fisher method. The
  Fisher recipes were validated against the MontePython likelihoods in
  2023
  (<xref alt="S. Casas et al., 2024" rid="ref-Casas2024" ref-type="bibr">S.
  Casas et al., 2024</xref>), and those likelihoods were in turn used as
  a backbone of current likelihoods such as
  <monospace>cloelike</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Bonici et al., 2026b" rid="ref-cloelike2026" ref-type="bibr">Bonici
  et al., 2026b</xref>), which uses <monospace>cloelib</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Bonici et al., 2026a" rid="ref-Bonici2026" ref-type="bibr">Bonici
  et al., 2026a</xref>). <monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> is
  therefore a very good way of having a fully independent but modern
  code as a backdrop, testing ground and validation tool.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The software is designed in a very modular way. The package layout
  follows the steps of a forecast:</p>
  <preformat>cosmicjellyfish/
  cosmology/     background, power spectra, backends, nuisance models
  LSSsurvey/     photometric, spectroscopic and intensity-mapping probes
  CMBsurvey/     CMB observables and covariances
  fishermatrix/  FisherMatrix driver, finite-difference and autodiff derivatives
  likelihood/    photometric and spectroscopic likelihoods, Nautilus sampler
  analysis/      Fisher operations, plotting, chains on top of Fisher contours
  configs/       survey specifications and Boltzmann-solver profiles (YAML)</preformat>
  <p><bold>Cosmology and backends.</bold> The cosmology module computes
  the background and other quantities, and it calls the different
  backends: CAMB
  (<xref alt="Lewis et al., 2000" rid="ref-Lewis2000" ref-type="bibr">Lewis
  et al., 2000</xref>) and CLASS
  (<xref alt="Blas et al., 2011" rid="ref-Blas2011" ref-type="bibr">Blas
  et al., 2011</xref>) through their Python wrappers, SYREN
  (<monospace>symbolic_pofk</monospace>), a fast symbolic emulator
  (<xref alt="Bartlett, Kammerer, et al., 2024" rid="ref-Bartlett2024a" ref-type="bibr">Bartlett,
  Kammerer, et al., 2024</xref>;
  <xref alt="Bartlett, Wandelt, et al., 2024" rid="ref-Bartlett2024b" ref-type="bibr">Bartlett,
  Wandelt, et al., 2024</xref>;
  <xref alt="Sui et al., 2025" rid="ref-Sui2025" ref-type="bibr">Sui et
  al., 2025</xref>), and <monospace>jax-cosmo</monospace>, a JAX
  implementation of the cosmological background and perturbation
  quantities
  (<xref alt="Campagne et al., 2023" rid="ref-Campagne2023" ref-type="bibr">Campagne
  et al., 2023</xref>). The backend is chosen with a single option,
  <monospace>options[&quot;code&quot;]</monospace>, which takes
  <monospace>&quot;camb&quot;</monospace>,
  <monospace>&quot;class&quot;</monospace>,
  <monospace>&quot;symbolic&quot;</monospace>,
  <monospace>&quot;jaxcosmo&quot;</monospace> or
  <monospace>&quot;external&quot;</monospace> (precomputed tables). This
  design makes it easy to implement other backends in the future, such
  as CosmoPower-JAX
  (<xref alt="Piras &amp; Spurio Mancini, 2023" rid="ref-Piras2023" ref-type="bibr">Piras
  &amp; Spurio Mancini, 2023</xref>) and DISCO-DJ
  (<xref alt="Hahn et al., 2024" rid="ref-Hahn2024" ref-type="bibr">Hahn
  et al., 2024</xref>), which are also differentiable.</p>
  <p><bold>Probes.</bold> The photometric, spectroscopic and
  intensity-mapping probes are coded independently in their own modules,
  and they work with any cosmological backend. The CMB part can in
  principle also be used with several backends, although for the moment
  we limit it to CAMB. In the near future we might integrate it with
  candl
  (<xref alt="Balkenhol et al., 2024" rid="ref-Balkenhol2024" ref-type="bibr">Balkenhol
  et al., 2024</xref>) to get easier access to CMB likelihoods.</p>
  <p><bold>Derivatives.</bold> The derivatives are also coded in a
  modular way, so that the different finite-difference methods
  (<monospace>3PT</monospace>, <monospace>4PT_FWD</monospace>,
  <monospace>POLY</monospace>, <monospace>STEM</monospace>) can be used
  independently, and JAX autodiff can be used instead by passing a
  derivative provider to <monospace>FisherMatrix</monospace>.</p>
  <p><bold>Likelihoods and sampling.</bold> We also have independent
  likelihood modules with several options, and modules that manage the
  runs with Nautilus
  (<xref alt="Lange, 2023" rid="ref-Lange2023" ref-type="bibr">Lange,
  2023</xref>). While Nautilus is the standard sampler for now, this can
  easily be extended to samplers such as pocoMC
  (<xref alt="Karamanis et al., 2022" rid="ref-Karamanis2022" ref-type="bibr">Karamanis
  et al., 2022</xref>) and others, and to gradient-based methods such as
  HMC that make use of autodiff gradients, as was done in
  <monospace>jax-cosmo</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Campagne et al., 2023" rid="ref-Campagne2023" ref-type="bibr">Campagne
  et al., 2023</xref>).</p>
  <p><bold>Analysis and plotting.</bold> Finally, the code provides a
  set of tools for analysing and plotting Fisher matrices:
  marginalising, combining, reparametrising and so on. Chains can easily
  be plotted on top of Fisher contours, using ChainConsumer
  (<xref alt="Hinton, 2016" rid="ref-Hinton2016" ref-type="bibr">Hinton,
  2016</xref>).</p>
</sec>
<sec id="usage-examples">
  <title>Usage examples</title>
  <sec id="a-euclid-photometric-fisher-matrix-combined-with-spectroscopic">
    <title>A Euclid photometric Fisher matrix, combined with
    spectroscopic</title>
    <p>A simple Euclid photometric Fisher matrix, combined with the
    spectroscopic one and marginalised over the nuisance parameters.
    With the symbolic backend the two forecasts take a few seconds on a
    laptop. The result is shown in
    <xref alt="[fig:probes]" rid="figU003Aprobes">[fig:probes]</xref>.</p>
    <code language="python">from cosmicjellyfish.analysis.fisher_operations import marginalise
from cosmicjellyfish.analysis.fishconsumer import make_triangle_plot
from cosmicjellyfish.fishermatrix import cosmicfish as cff

fiducial = {&quot;Omegam&quot;: 0.32, &quot;Omegab&quot;: 0.05, &quot;h&quot;: 0.67,
            &quot;ns&quot;: 0.96, &quot;sigma8&quot;: 0.815584}
freepars = {&quot;Omegam&quot;: 0.01, &quot;h&quot;: 0.01, &quot;sigma8&quot;: 0.01}  # relative steps


def options(photo, spectro):
    return {
        &quot;code&quot;: &quot;symbolic&quot;,  # or &quot;camb&quot; / &quot;class&quot;
        &quot;accuracy&quot;: 1,
        &quot;nonlinear&quot;: True,
        &quot;derivatives&quot;: &quot;3PT&quot;,
        &quot;results_dir&quot;: &quot;results&quot;,
        &quot;outroot&quot;: &quot;example&quot;,
        &quot;survey_name&quot;: &quot;Euclid&quot;,
        &quot;survey_name_photo&quot;: photo,
        &quot;survey_name_spectro&quot;: spectro,
    }


photo = cff.FisherMatrix(
    fiducialpars=fiducial,
    freepars=freepars,
    options=options(&quot;Euclid-Photometric-ISTF-Pessimistic&quot;, False),
    observables=[&quot;WL&quot;, &quot;GCph&quot;],
    cosmoModel=&quot;LCDM&quot;,
    surveyName=&quot;Euclid&quot;,
).compute()

spectro = cff.FisherMatrix(
    fiducialpars=fiducial,
    freepars=freepars,
    options=options(False, &quot;Euclid-Spectroscopic-ISTF-Pessimistic&quot;),
    observables=[&quot;GCsp&quot;],
    cosmoModel=&quot;LCDM&quot;,
    surveyName=&quot;Euclid&quot;,
).compute()

combined = photo + spectro  # nuisance parameters of both probes are kept
reduced = marginalise(combined, [&quot;Omegam&quot;, &quot;h&quot;, &quot;sigma8&quot;])
print(reduced.get_confidence_bounds())  # marginalised 1-sigma errors

make_triangle_plot(
    fishers=[spectro, photo, combined],
    fisher_labels=[&quot;GCsp&quot;, &quot;WL+GCph&quot;, &quot;WL+GCph+GCsp&quot;],
    params=[&quot;Omegam&quot;, &quot;h&quot;, &quot;sigma8&quot;],
    savefile=&quot;euclid_triangle.png&quot;,
)</code>
    <p>Another experiment is added in the same way, by choosing another
    shipped specification (for example
    <monospace>&quot;SKAO-Spectroscopic-Redbook&quot;</monospace>) and
    summing the resulting Fisher matrix.</p>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Marginalised constraints from Euclid spectroscopic
      clustering (GCsp), the photometric probes (WL+GCph) and their
      combination, for flat <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[\Lambda]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>CDM
      with the IST:F pessimistic specifications and the symbolic
      backend. This is the output of the example
      above.<styled-content id="figU003Aprobes"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="fig1_euclid_probes.png" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
  <sec id="a-nautilus-chain-that-matches-the-fisher-matrix">
    <title>A Nautilus chain that matches the Fisher matrix</title>
    <p>A simple example running a chain with Nautilus from the same
    settings as the Fisher forecast. The sampler writes the weighted
    chain, a resumable checkpoint and a metadata file, which are then
    loaded and plotted on top of the Fisher contours.</p>
    <code language="python">from cosmicjellyfish.likelihood import NautilusSampler

config = {
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;wl_demo&quot;,
    &quot;fiducial&quot;: fiducial,  # as above
    &quot;observables&quot;: [&quot;WL&quot;],
    &quot;options&quot;: {
        &quot;code&quot;: &quot;symbolic&quot;,
        &quot;cosmo_model&quot;: &quot;LCDM&quot;,
        &quot;accuracy&quot;: 1,
        &quot;nonlinear&quot;: True,
        &quot;ell_sampling&quot;: 25,  # coarse grid for speed
        &quot;results_dir&quot;: &quot;results&quot;,
        &quot;outroot&quot;: &quot;wl_demo&quot;,
        &quot;survey_name&quot;: &quot;Euclid&quot;,
        &quot;survey_name_photo&quot;: &quot;Euclid-Photometric-ISTF-Pessimistic&quot;,
        &quot;survey_name_spectro&quot;: False,
    },
    &quot;priors&quot;: {&quot;Omegam&quot;: [0.28, 0.36], &quot;sigma8&quot;: [0.75, 0.87]},
    &quot;sampler_settings&quot;: {&quot;n_live&quot;: 500, &quot;n_networks&quot;: 1, &quot;n_batch&quot;: 50,
                         &quot;pool&quot;: 8, &quot;n_eff&quot;: 2000, &quot;verbose&quot;: False},
}

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:  # required for pool &gt; 1: workers are spawned
    NautilusSampler(config).run()</code>
    <code language="python">from cosmicjellyfish.analysis.fishconsumer import (
    load_Nautilus_chains_from_txt,
    make_triangle_plot,
)
from cosmicjellyfish.likelihood.sampler import load_chain_metadata

chain_file, sampled_fiducial, metadata, labels = load_chain_metadata(
    &quot;chains/chains_wl_demo&quot;
)
columns = open(chain_file).readline().lstrip(&quot;# &quot;).split()[:-2]
chain = load_Nautilus_chains_from_txt(chain_file, columns)

make_triangle_plot(
    chains=[chain], chain_labels=[&quot;Nautilus&quot;],
    fishers=[fisher], fisher_labels=[&quot;Fisher&quot;],  # a WL Fisher matrix
    params=columns, truth_values=sampled_fiducial, param_labels=labels,
    savefile=&quot;wl_chain_vs_fisher.png&quot;,
)</code>
    <p>A chain that samples only the cosmological parameters has to be
    compared with the Fisher matrix at fixed nuisance parameters
    (<monospace>eliminate_parameters</monospace>); when the nuisance
    parameters are sampled as well, it is compared with the marginalised
    one, as in
    <xref alt="[fig:nautilus]" rid="figU003Anautilus">[fig:nautilus]</xref>.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="a-jax-autodiff-fisher-matrix-against-the-standard-one">
    <title>A JAX autodiff Fisher matrix against the standard one</title>
    <p>Finally, a JAX autodiff Fisher matrix against the standard
    finite-difference one, with the same <monospace>jaxcosmo</monospace>
    theory. This needs the optional extra,
    <monospace>pip install &quot;cosmicjellyfish[jaxcosmo]&quot;</monospace>.</p>
    <code language="python">from cosmicjellyfish.fishermatrix.cosmicfish import FisherMatrix
from cosmicjellyfish.fishermatrix.jaxcosmo_derivatives import JaxAutodiffProvider

fiducial = {&quot;10^9As&quot;: 2.1, &quot;Omegam&quot;: 0.32, &quot;Omegab&quot;: 0.05,
            &quot;h&quot;: 0.67, &quot;ns&quot;: 0.96, &quot;mnu&quot;: 0.0}
freepars = {name: 0.01 for name in (&quot;10^9As&quot;, &quot;Omegam&quot;, &quot;Omegab&quot;, &quot;h&quot;)}


def forecast(provider, outroot):
    options = {  # a fresh dictionary for each forecast
        &quot;code&quot;: &quot;jaxcosmo&quot;,
        &quot;accuracy&quot;: 1,
        &quot;nonlinear&quot;: True,
        &quot;derivatives&quot;: &quot;3PT&quot;,
        &quot;results_dir&quot;: &quot;results&quot;,
        &quot;outroot&quot;: outroot,
        &quot;survey_name_photo&quot;: &quot;Euclid-Photometric-ISTF-Pessimistic&quot;,
    }
    return FisherMatrix(
        fiducialpars=fiducial,
        freepars=freepars,
        options=options,
        observables=[&quot;GCph&quot;, &quot;WL&quot;],
        cosmoModel=&quot;LCDM&quot;,
        surveyName=&quot;Euclid&quot;,
        derivative_provider=provider,
    ).compute()


fisher_ad = forecast(JaxAutodiffProvider(), &quot;photo_ad&quot;)  # autodiff (jax.jacfwd)
fisher_fd = forecast(None, &quot;photo_fd&quot;)  # standard finite differences

print(fisher_ad.get_confidence_bounds() / fisher_fd.get_confidence_bounds())</code>
    <p>For this setup the marginalised errors of the two routes agree to
    better than 0.4% for all 17 cosmological and nuisance parameters
    (<xref alt="[fig:autodiff]" rid="figU003Aautodiff">[fig:autodiff]</xref>).
    When the finite differences are taken on the same JAX observable
    (<monospace>JaxFiniteDifferenceProvider</monospace>), the agreement
    improves to better than 0.05%, and the two Fisher matrices differ by
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[1.8\times10^{-5}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mn>1.8</mml:mn><mml:mo>×</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>5</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
    in <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\max|\Delta F|/\max|F|]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>max</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mml:mi><mml:mi>F</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mi>max</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:mi>F</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
    The remaining difference is therefore due to the two implementations
    of the observable and not to the derivative method.</p>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Marginalised errors from finite differences relative
      to those from automatic differentiation, for the Euclid WL+GCph
      forecast with the <monospace>jaxcosmo</monospace> backend. Filled
      circles: finite differences of the same JAX observable. Open
      squares: finite differences of the standard NumPy
      observable.<styled-content id="figU003Aautodiff"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="fig2_autodiff_ratios.png" />
    </fig>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="validation-and-performance">
  <title>Validation and performance</title>
  <p><bold>Validation.</bold> The CAMB–CLASS validation and the
  MontePython validation are documented in the validation dashboard
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.santicasas.space/cosmicjellyfish/dashboard/">https://www.santicasas.space/cosmicjellyfish/dashboard/</ext-link>),
  with all the scripts and results in the data folders of the DagsHub
  repository
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://dagshub.com/santiagocasas/cjfp-data">https://dagshub.com/santiagocasas/cjfp-data</ext-link>).
  The suite covers 25 cases: photometric and spectroscopic Euclid probes
  for <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>CDM
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[w_0w_a]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mi>a</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>CDM,
  with and without massive neutrinos and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N_{\mathrm{eff}}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">e</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">f</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">f</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  following the settings of S. Casas et al.
  (<xref alt="2024" rid="ref-Casas2024" ref-type="bibr">2024</xref>) and
  Euclid Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al.
  (<xref alt="2025" rid="ref-Archidiacono2025" ref-type="bibr">2025</xref>).
  <monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> itself (formerly
  <monospace>cosmicfishpie</monospace>) was validated in several Euclid
  papers and beyond
  (<xref alt="S. Casas et al., 2024" rid="ref-Casas2024" ref-type="bibr">S.
  Casas et al., 2024</xref>;
  <xref alt="Euclid Collaboration et al., 2020" rid="ref-Blanchard2020" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Euclid Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025" rid="ref-Archidiacono2025" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025</xref>).</p>
  <p><bold>Performance.</bold> For a Euclid photometric forecast, the
  default profiles take about 30 s (CAMB) and 111 s (CLASS), against 138
  s and 186 s for the high-precision profiles of Euclid Collaboration,
  Archidiacono, et al.
  (<xref alt="2025" rid="ref-Archidiacono2025" ref-type="bibr">2025</xref>).
  The performance has been improving over the years. With the symbolic
  module we now obtain Fisher matrices in a few seconds, also for models
  such as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[w_0w_a]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mi>a</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>CDM:
  with seven cosmological parameters and all nuisance parameters, the
  WL+GCph Fisher matrix (20 parameters) takes about 8 s and the GCsp one
  (15 parameters) about 2.5 s <bold>[TODO: measured on two cores of a
  cloud VM; replace by your own hardware]</bold>. With CAMB and CLASS we
  provide several levels of accuracy and precision, and the Fisher
  matrices take less or more time depending on that.</p>
  <p><bold>Chains against Fisher matrices.</bold> We have also validated
  our chains against our own Fisher matrices and shown that they agree
  (<xref alt="[fig:nautilus]" rid="figU003Anautilus">[fig:nautilus]</xref>).
  For Euclid WL, GCsp and their combination, with all nuisance
  parameters sampled, the marginalised Nautilus errors on
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Omega_{\mathrm{m}}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">m</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\sigma_8]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>σ</mml:mi><mml:mn>8</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  are within 5%, 0.4% and 1.3% of the Fisher ones, respectively, and the
  posterior means lie within 0.1<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\sigma]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>σ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  of the fiducial values.</p>
  <p>The timings of these runs are interesting in themselves. With the
  symbolic backend, 64 Nautilus workers on JURECA,
  <monospace>n_live=500</monospace> and a target of 2000 effective
  samples, the WL chain (5 sampled dimensions) took 17 minutes, the GCsp
  chain (10 dimensions) 22 minutes and the joint WL+GCsp chain (13
  dimensions) 1 hour and 45 minutes. A full posterior for the Euclid
  primary probes, including nuisance parameters, is thus obtained in
  about 18, 24 and 112 core-hours, respectively, while the corresponding
  Fisher matrices take seconds. This makes it affordable to check the
  Gaussian approximation case by case, instead of assuming it.</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>Fisher forecasts (dark, thick lines) against Nautilus
    posteriors (light, thin lines) for Euclid weak lensing,
    spectroscopic clustering and their combination, with the symbolic
    backend and all nuisance parameters marginalised in both
    cases.<styled-content id="figU003Anautilus"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="fig3_fisher_vs_nautilus.png" />
  </fig>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>As <monospace>cosmicfishpie</monospace>, the code has been used in
  about ten publications. It started with the Euclid IST:F forecasts for
  the spectroscopic and photometric probes
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration et al., 2020" rid="ref-Blanchard2020" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration et al., 2020</xref>), and it was then used in several
  Euclid papers forecasting extended models, such as
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[f(R)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>f</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>R</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  gravity
  (<xref alt="S. Casas et al., 2026" rid="ref-Casas2026fR" ref-type="bibr">S.
  Casas et al., 2026</xref>) and scalar-field and other modified-gravity
  models
  (<xref alt="Frusciante et al., 2024" rid="ref-Frusciante2024" ref-type="bibr">Frusciante
  et al., 2024</xref>), and models of inflation
  (<xref alt="Ballardini et al., 2024" rid="ref-Ballardini2024" ref-type="bibr">Ballardini
  et al., 2024</xref>) <bold>[TODO: confirm that these three are the
  papers you mean]</bold>. It served in the paper validating the
  MontePython likelihoods
  (<xref alt="S. Casas et al., 2024" rid="ref-Casas2024" ref-type="bibr">S.
  Casas et al., 2024</xref>) and in the Euclid paper on the sensitivity
  to neutrino cosmology, which used MontePython and
  <monospace>cosmicfishpie</monospace> together
  (<xref alt="Euclid Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025" rid="ref-Archidiacono2025" ref-type="bibr">Euclid
  Collaboration, Archidiacono, et al., 2025</xref>). It was also used in
  the papers on radio <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\times]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>×</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  optical probes with SKAO
  (<xref alt="Santiago Casas et al., 2023" rid="ref-Casas2023SKAO" ref-type="bibr">Santiago
  Casas et al., 2023</xref>) and in a few other works <bold>[TODO: cite
  the remaining papers]</bold>.</p>
  <p>The Boltzmann-solver precision profiles validated in those works
  are shipped with the package, and the validation dashboard, the
  posterior chains and the cluster workflows are public, so that every
  comparison quoted here can be reproduced from a recorded source
  revision.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="documentation">
  <title>Documentation</title>
  <p>The documentation
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.santicasas.space/cosmicjellyfish/docs/">https://www.santicasas.space/cosmicjellyfish/docs/</ext-link>)
  contains an installation guide, a first forecast, a user guide on
  backends, observables, derivative methods and likelihood sampling, the
  validation reports and an API reference. Eight tutorial notebooks in
  the repository cover the cosmology interface, spectroscopic and
  photometric forecasts, the CMB, Fisher post-processing, Nautilus
  sampling, autodiff against finite differences, and forecasts in
  intermediate parameters. A migration guide describes the change from
  <monospace>cosmicfishpie</monospace>, whose import path keeps working
  as a deprecated alias.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="availability">
  <title>Availability</title>
  <p><monospace>cosmicjellyfish</monospace> is released under the Apache
  2.0 licence. The source code is hosted at
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/santiagocasas/cosmicjellyfish">https://github.com/santiagocasas/cosmicjellyfish</ext-link>
  and the package is installed with
  <monospace>pip install cosmicjellyfish</monospace> for Python 3.10 to
  3.12. Validation data and workflows are available at
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://dagshub.com/santiagocasas/cjfp-data">https://dagshub.com/santiagocasas/cjfp-data</ext-link>.</p>
</sec>
